Subject: Re: Current P?S/8 support level specifics.
To: P?S/8 <Unknown>


This will be a clearer explanation of the source file status of P?S/8 as of 
this writing.

It is unfortunate that not all of the P/S/8 source files are currently 
available [even to me!].  There are no valid excuses, but 20/20 hindsight 
cannot be applied.  It is hoped that all are either replaced or recovered 
without undue effort.

1) The main source files of P?S/8 as of 23-Feb-1988 have been fully 
recovered.  This was the typical "portable" collection of source files as 
used by P?S/8 developers on an ongoing basis.  The primary reason this 
collection is not a wider set of source files is simply due to expediency.

To create P/S/8 from the source files, you need a working OS/8 system with 
a decent enough version of PAL8.  This generally means PAL8 V10D, which is 
a few minor patches past the standard release from OS/8 V3D [3Q].
Eventually this would become PAL8 Version B0 [and the companion CREF 
Version B0 from OS/278 V2.  These two programs are the best [and final] DEC 
releases of these two programs and are known to work on all models to 
within a nitpick quirk.  Due to some internal minor changes to keyboard 
handling, they can be an extremely obscure problem with timing of buffered 
Control-Z characters when using one of the TTY: handlers.  Consider an 
assembly where a few lines are needed to me entered hy hand followed by a 
series of files that are dependent on conditional assembly parameters.  In 
general, this could be as little as a single line of input such as

FOO=1
^Z

Due to the nature of OS/8, one of the input "files? can be to take input 
from the TTY: [or KL8E] handler needed only during the first pass.  [Note: 
If the input during the listing phase of assembly is limited to ONLY a 
Control-Z, the output will not be affected; this is generally desirable to 
obtain a listing, etc.  All of this works correctly in PAL8 Version B0.

That said, only in the earlier releases is it possible to type multiple 
Control-Z characters all at once.  The purpose would be for the assembly to 
automatically continue into the subsequent pass[s].  Due to some changes in 
Version B0, it will be necessary to enter the Contril-Z character at the 
start of each pass.  Thus, it is not possible to buffer the characters; you 
have to be present to enter the subsequent Control-Z characters when they 
are needed.  [No attempt has been made to correct this obscure behavior; 
clearly the problem is that something was added that clears the keyboard 
flag not used in prior versions].

for all other purposes, these two programs appear to be 100% compatible, 
fix all prior bugs [and perhaps some still in prior versions!] and appear 
to be fully compatible with the "straignt" -8 and all newer models.

The above incidents were notices during the development of Kermit-12 where 
there are numerous conditionals that can easily be over-ridden in various 
ways.  To avoid this problem, the Kermit-12 package includes a small file 
PARAM.PAL which can be used to implement all such options as well as a few 
that would be quite unwieldy and error-prone by the problematic method.  By 
the use of certain conditional assembly tricks, the file does not change 
any unintended aspects of the listing file created for a custom version.

Clearly, few people ever have contact with this obscurity, and it was more 
important to know that where the P?S/8 sources were taken to, it could be 
expected that a viable PAL8 be available to assemble the P?S/8 source code 
on the target system.

In general, the "portable" collection of the P/S/8 source files became the 
so-called "A" collection.and the "B" collection.  This is an arbitrary 
split of "all" of the files so that the entire collection could reside on 
an RK05 pack [such as RKA1: and RKB1:] to allow certain OS/8 BATCH programs 
to be run that depended on the relevant specifics.  Thus, there are copying 
BATCH files to essentially transfer the two collection "halves" from say a 
collection of three specific DECtapes to RKA1: and another collection of 
three DECtapes to RKB1:.  [Note: Much of the actual development in those 
days was done by hand-carrying the six DECTapes and some additional 
DECtapes for sundry other purposes to an installation based on either a 
pair of RK05 drives, or the DSD-240-8 drive where the lower disk is exactly 
the same size as a pair of RK05 drives, and P?S/8 is configured to occupy 
the upper pair of drives to allow P?S/8 logical units 0 through 7 in a like 
manner.

While there are a few different RK05 configurations for P?S/8 [not all of 
which were ever actually needed at the time[ the DSD-240-8 configuration 
was essentially identical to OS/8's allocation for RK05 disks as the clear 
"natural" configuration due to the disk geometry, etc.

This allows P?S/8 to support eight logical drives each exactly 3130 blocks 
long; the system device handler is used to access the entire drive complex.

Note: The other possibilities for the actual RK8E/RK8F support consist of a 
few possibilities, some of which require some additional research to 
implement:

1) All eight logical units on one drive.  Each logical unit is about the 
size of a single DECtape.  This is only useful on a single RK05 drive 
system.  [Note: Due to temporary restrictions of P?S/8 PAL, the /X 
cross-reference option cannot be used unless logical unit 7 is available; 
additionally it is not totally implemented, but enough of the option exists 
to obtain the actual statement numbers that will exist when this assembler 
section is completed; this depends on changes being made to P?S/8 itself 
that will be available in Version 9A.]

While this is a nice fast system, it is limited for working with such as 
extended-length files, and certain difficulties in converting files between 
OS/8 and P?S/8.

Using the standard EXTENDED RK8E bootstrap, this system can be booted to 
any drive 0, 1, 2 or 3.

2) Four units are on one drive.  Each logical unit is half the storage of 
an OS/8 equivalent.  Since by nature, inter-system file conversion can only 
be done to the first "half" of an OS/8 device, this configuration is quite 
useful for development purposes.  Note: It is quite rare to find a single 
PDP-8 source file larger than 1/2 of one RKAx: or RKBx: area on an RK05 
cartridge.  Thus, to date this has not been a problem.]  OS8CON and future 
BINCON conversions are easily carried out to the even logical units.

Using the standard EXTENDED RK8E bootstrap, this system can be booted to 
drives 0 or 4.  The scope of this system can be either RK05 drives 0 and 1 
OR 2 and 3.  P?S/8 can be booted to drive 4.directly.  Definitions exist to 
boot to other logical units by the use of extensions to the extended 
standard bootstrap, but this is rarely actually done.  [Note: Using 
equivalent design logic the above system with all logical units on the same 
RK05 cartridge, it is possible to boot to ANY logical unit.]

3) Two logical units are on one drive.  this is the configuration that 
completely matches OS/8.  This is the typical configuration that was used 
when developing P?S/8 for RK8E/RK8F..  Extensions to the standard extended 
RK bootstrap allows booting to any logical unit.  The 8 logical units of 
P?S/8 are addressed by the P?S/8 system handler, and each corresponds to an 
OS/8 RKAx: or RKBx: device.  [Note: This is the default configuration for 
the present.]

4) This is a tentative specification for support of up to EIGHT RK05 
drives.  Each RK05 cartridge is a logical unit that extends to block 
031277.  Logical units are larger than what OS/8 can address in a single 
device.
This configuration has not been developed [but is easy to program for] 
simply because there was no reason to require it until know.  The P?S/8 
SHELL will define an optional system overlay where all file access is by 
non-system handlers that support double-precision block numbers; as such, 
the P?S/8 SHELL file structure can acces as much as 2048 times as much 
storage as OS/8, yet only requiring a since 128-word input or output 
buffer.  The system device handler is still limited to the first 4096 
blocks; the only requirement is that all of the actual SHELL components 
reside in this area including the SHELL directory.  To protect all of the 
basic P?S/8 system components, the SHELL directory includes protected area 
support via attribute bits including system and unmovable.  The SHELL 
intercommunicates internally using the system device handler.  All SHELL 
file structures are accessed by non-system handlers loaded using the system 
device handler to accomplish this.

Note: P?S/8 already supports non-system device handlers; however, the only 
program that can make use of them is the stop-gap program P?S/8 BLKCPY. 
 This is a copy or compare anything to anything program specifying absolute 
blocks and will be an invaluable tool to develop the SHELL.  P?S/8 Version 
9A will be the minimum requirement to develop the SHELL as certain system 
kernel bits are being defined requiring slight changes to all existing 
basic P?S/8 programs.  [Short answer:  I found SEVEN free bits!  They were 
overlooked until recently.  The change is minor and always in once-only 
initialization code.  The low-order five bits must be isolated; in Version 
8Z, it is assumed the other bits are all zero, etc.]  These new bits will 
be used to finally implement the replacement system date algorithm as well 
as an important system definition that only pertains to the SHELL 
configuration.

The notion of an additional four RK05 drives depends on the hardware being 
the somewhat obscure RKS8E [I have one in need of some repair.]  The RK05 
drives are no longer hardware-accessed using the RK11-C protocol, and 
replaced using the RK11-D protocol.

The P?S/8 RK handles have a lot of free space within the handler due to the 
expansion of the definition of the system device handler to as much as 
eight pages in the system device handler extended memory extension.  When 
the RK system was first developed, only four pages were required.  Newer 
system device challenged have caused this specification to be doubled up to 
an entire 1K of the highest existent memory field location x6000-x7777 
where x is the highest memory field [at least 1] Unlike OS/8, P?S/8 uses 
the HIGHEST extended memory field; OS/8 uses the LOWEST field 1 thus 
forever preventing a wider range of 15-bit addressable memory without 
restrictions [and many ugly kludges in such as the FORTRAN IV run-time 
system.  These essentially try to do poorly what P?S/8 does as designed.  I 
broached this with my friends at DEC, and they agreed the P?S/8 method was 
superior, but don't expect ANYTHING about OS/8 to ever be changed!  [And 
here we are addressing this issue decades later!]

What is needed is some bullet-proof code to determine if the device is 
actually an RKS8E and not an ordinary RK8E/RK8F.  [Note: The RK8F is a 
modified RK8E for use in the DW8E.  this is NOT a programming 
consideration.]  The RKS8E defines an additional bit of disk drive 
addressing, but it must be clear how to determine if the hardware actually 
exists, lest there be anomaly between such as drive 0 and drive 4, etc. 
 [Note: There are apparently obscure OS/8 RKS8E handlers for the additional 
drives.  They are a total copout that depends on the user NOT using the 
handlers where the hardware doesn't exist!  Attempts to do so will likely 
corrupt the corresponding LOWER drive!]

Eventually for a brief period, little of this "sneeakernet" operations were 
performed, as there was available MDC8 in my home system.  In this system, 
OS/8 devices are the maximum of 4096 records.

At some point, most of the lost files were relegated to a "seventh" 
DECtape, but in fact this was not quite viable, even when 3300 block 
DECtapes were being used.  No copies of this seventh [or eighth] tape have 
been recovered to date] and it was theorized that all of the problematic 
devices were lost.  Below is the status on a device by device basis.

Prior to 1983, a vane attempt was made to have all devices supported in a 
monstrous single file.  [Again, 20/20 hindsight need not apply; this was 
corrected, but not completely.]

A key problem with having the giant file PQSMON.PAL was the confusion of 
many conditionals coupled with the quirky source length restrictions of 
PAL8 [part of your code can be ignored and you may not know it!].

Additionally, the hit-and-miss support for the Logical Console Overlay had 
to be firmed up.  The problem is this:

Each system device handler has to provide a pair of patch lists for the 
handler to be carried out when the Logical Console Overlay is coming up OR 
being disabled [to restore the handler to the normal code].

These patch definition implement such as intercepting the standard system 
HALT to instead be trapped in the Overlay to allow user interaction [retry, 
abort] and display of all of the latest system call parameters, etc.

In systems such as the PDP-12 LINCtape, a 4K system handler cannot 
over-ride the automatic initial search direction guess of always in reverse 
[which is admittedly usually correct, but as much as 40 percent of the time 
this is not true.  Performance can be materially speeded up, etc.]

When the Logical Console Overlay is enabled on a PDP-12 LINCtape system, 
other parts of the handler intercept to instead access a last-transferred 
block on the corresponding logical unit.  If appropriate, the initial 
search direction can be forced to forward using MTB instructions in 
extended operatons mode.  There isn't enough room in the basic 4K handler 
to accomplish this, but if extended memory is available and the Overlay is 
enabled, the handler performce can be palpably improved.

Thus, the full definition of what compromises all of the code for any 
particular device [what OS/8 would dub a "system device handler" has to 
include these short patch lists defined in the system device handler 
address space, and some field relocatable interception routines defined in 
the Overlay address space.

I really do not recall, but I suspect at some point, the source code of the 
Overlay also had conditionals within it for this purpose, but the newer 
source code does not.  This function has been added to the source code of 
all of the fully supported devices, notable ones listed below.  Feel free 
to inquire regarding other devices not mentioned.

A recently recovered file from 1983 appears to be the last gasp of an 
attempt to have all of the major components of P?S/8 not only in memory at 
the same time, but also from the output of a single assembly.

Note:  The creation of a P?S/8 starter system is to assemble these same 
components [and a few additional ones] into individual binary modules where 
a system generation routine [essentially a string of calls to the system 
device handler] is used to write out all of the components from memory to 
the designated target system blocks.  Programs are assembled such that they 
appear to be in the address space they execute in, but are binary loaded in 
buffers determined by the assembly process as defined for system 
generation.  However, in the original oversized version, the only thing 
gained was local definitions followed through the assembly process being a 
single unit.

This was later corrected in a far more globally acceptable way:

All global definitions must originate in the keyboard monitor assembly, 
which today is devoid of actual handler-related code [just templates and 
some ZBLOCK sections].  In a few places, some definitions actually 
originate in the Logical Console Overlay, and if they change there, they 
must be updated in the main PQSMON source file even if not actually used 
there.

A TECO macro has been written [called GLOBAL.TEC] [which was also used in 
the creation of the PR Newswire project development] that creates a symbol 
table from the listing file of the assembly in memory, but only for 
specific symbols that may be defined within a source file within special 
delimters:

//  SPECIAL SYSTEMS SYMBOLS

equates here

//  END OF SPECIAL SYSTEMS SYMBOLS

The macro actually only checks for the existence of the first section of 
comments starting with // followed by equate states [such as SBOOT= 7600] 
ended with a second occurrence of the // comment.

The only restriction is that all lines within the area have to be either 
blank or equate statements.

The macro is passed an assembler listing file [only the symbols are needed] 
and the target source file to be updated..

Thus, it is imperative the symbol table of PQSMON define all symbols [which 
means a few kludges are used, such as definitions that actually arise from 
the Logical Console Overlay and perhaps the P/S/8 BIN utility, now that 
these are all separate assemblies.  Fortunately, these external definitions 
are not likely to change by nature.

Once all compatible components have been processed, the files can be 
assembled individually and then each can take its proper binary place in 
the system generation process.  12K or perhaps 16K is required; future 
changes will limit this to requiring 12K or perhaps less [depending upon 
the availability of a proper implementation of the DTORG directive].

The only problem with what was accomplished is that certain system devices 
were "left behind" and in a few cases, never defined for an overlay.

It was feared some were entirely lost such as the TD8E system that uses 
extended memory for which I only had some vague odd recollections of some 
optimizations over the code as used in most of the OS/8 handlers [which is 
actually pretty poor].

Eventually a long-forgotten existent TD8E w/MR8E-C ROM system was 
recovered, and actually booted.  It is now theorized that a serious but was 
never addressed, as there was little further contact with actual TD8E 
hardware.

The bug in this system is that there is an attempt to overlay the code with 
a copy of itself take off the DECtape from the corresponding blocks.  this 
technique works entirely well IF the TC01/08 DECtape controller is in 
effect because of the use of DMA.

In the TD8E ROM system, unfortunately a checksum temporary is designated by 
the ROM; it cannot be moved to a "safe place".  As such, any attempt to 
modify the actual block contents renders the system unbootable.  [I needed 
this tape for an expedient piurpose, plus I have a "negative association" 
with this tape because of the personnel involved, but this is beyond any 
technical discussion.

I have disassembled other P?S/8 system components before [as well as code I 
never wrote; the cosmetic quality and commentary generally exceed that of 
the original author's.].  this is part of a large document with a lot of 
good documentation that should be split-off and word-processed, however, 
the code is confirmed as 100% correct for an old release of P?S/8 circa 
1976 for TC01/08.

As such, I was confident I could recover all of the code on this TD8E w/ROM 
system and eventually did reproduce the source code [including the ROM 
itself].  It will be interesting to see the "quality" of this disassembly 
compared to the original code [now recently possible!].

I have located the bug and made a partial start on solving the problem, 
tabling this for time's sake; the SIMH simulator allows debugging the 
results, etc.

With regard to TD8E-related issues, it is apprarently now nearly a complete 
recovery, except that it is known the code did work with the then-current 
VT program, which was the non-configurable forerunner of the Logical 
Console Overlay.

Thus, I have to disassemble that code to determine what are the appropriate 
interactive intercepts to apply and the appropriate error messages for the 
generic benefit of the Overlay.

For brevity I will close at this time, next time a few more "lost boy" 
stories and a final summary.

cjl



