e.<br>This configuration has not been developed [but is easy to program for=
] simply because there was no reason to require it until know. &nbsp;The P?=
S/8 SHELL will define an optional system overlay where all file access is b=
y non-system handlers that support double-precision block numbers; as such,=
 the P?S/8 SHELL file structure can acces as much as 2048 times as much sto=
rage as OS/8, yet only requiring a since 128-word input or output buffer. &=
nbsp;The system device handler is still limited to the first 4096 blocks; t=
he only requirement is that all of the actual SHELL components reside in th=
is area including the SHELL directory. &nbsp;To protect all of the basic P?=
S/8 system components, the SHELL directory includes protected area support =
via attribute bits including system and unmovable. &nbsp;The SHELL intercom=
municates internally using the system device handler. &nbsp;All SHELL file =
structures are accessed by non-system handlers loaded using the system devi=
ce handler to accomplish this.<br><br>Note: P?S/8 already supports non-syst=
em device handlers; however, the only program that can make use of them is =
the stop-gap program P?S/8 BLKCPY. &nbsp;This is a copy or compare anything=
 to anything program specifying absolute blocks and will be an invaluable t=
ool to develop the SHELL. &nbsp;P?S/8 Version 9A will be the minimum requir=
ement to develop the SHELL as certain system kernel bits are being defined =
requiring slight changes to all existing basic P?S/8 programs. &nbsp;[Short=
 answer: &nbsp;I found SEVEN free bits! &nbsp;They were overlooked until re=
cently. &nbsp;The change is minor and always in once-only initialization co=
de. &nbsp;The low-order five bits must be isolated; in Version 8Z, it is as=
sumed the other bits are all zero, etc.] &nbsp;These new bits will be used =
to finally implement the replacement system date algorithm as well as an im=
portant system definition that only pertains to the SHELL configuration.<br=
><br>The notion of an additional four RK05 drives depends on the hardware b=
eing the somewhat obscure RKS8E [I have one in need of some repair.] &nbsp;=
The RK05 drives are no longer hardware-accessed using the RK11-C protocol, =
and replaced using the RK11-D protocol.<br><br>The P?S/8 RK handles have a =
lot of free space within the handler due to the expansion of the definition=
 of the system device handler to as much as eight pages in the system devic=
e handler extended memory extension. &nbsp;When the RK system was first dev=
eloped, only four pages were required. &nbsp;Newer system device challenged=
 have caused this specification to be doubled up to an entire 1K of the hig=
hest existent memory field location x6000-x7777 where x is the highest memo=
ry field [at least 1] Unlike OS/8, P?S/8 uses the HIGHEST extended memory f=
ield; OS/8 uses the LOWEST field 1 thus forever preventing a wider range of=
 15-bit addressable memory without restrictions [and many ugly kludges in s=
uch as the FORTRAN IV run-time system. &nbsp;These essentially try to do po=
orly what P?S/8 does as designed. &nbsp;I broached this with my friends at =
DEC, and they agreed the P?S/8 method was superior, but don't expect ANYTHI=
NG about OS/8 to ever be changed! &nbsp;[And here we are addressing this is=
sue decades later!]<br><br>What is needed is some bullet-proof code to dete=
rmine if the device is actually an RKS8E and not an ordinary RK8E/RK8F. &nb=
sp;[Note: The RK8F is a modified RK8E for use in the DW8E. &nbsp;this is NO=
T a programming consideration.] &nbsp;The RKS8E defines an additional bit o=
f disk drive addressing, but it must be clear how to determine if the hardw=
are actually exists, lest there be anomaly between such as drive 0 and driv=
e 4, etc. &nbsp;[Note: There are apparently obscure OS/8 RKS8E handlers for=
 the additional drives. &nbsp;They are a total copout that depends on the u=
ser NOT using the handlers where the hardware doesn't exist! &nbsp;Attempts=
 to do so will likely corrupt the corresponding LOWER drive!]<br><br>Eventu=
ally for a brief period, little of this "sneeakernet" operations were perfo=
rmed, as there was available MDC8 in my home system. &nbsp;In this system, =
OS/8 devices are the maximum of 4096 records.<br><br>At some point, most of=
 the lost files were relegated to a "seventh" DECtape, but in fact this was=
 not quite viable, even when 3300 block DECtapes were being used. &nbsp;No =
copies of this seventh [or eighth] tape have been recovered to date] and it=
 was theorized that all of the problematic devices were lost. &nbsp;Below i=
s the status on a device by device basis.<br><br>Prior to 1983, a vane atte=
mpt was made to have all devices supported in a monstrous single file. &nbs=
p;[Again, 20/20 hindsight need not apply; this was corrected, but not compl=
etely.]<br><br>A key problem with having the giant file PQSMON.PAL was the =
confusion of many conditionals coupled with the quirky source length restri=
ctions of PAL8 [part of your code can be ignored and you may not know it!].=
<br><br>Additionally, the hit-and-miss support for the Logical Console Over=
lay had to be firmed up. &nbsp;The problem is this:<br><br>Each system devi=
ce handler has to provide a pair of patch lists for the handler to be carri=
ed out when the Logical Console Overlay is coming up OR being disabled [to =
restore the handler to the normal code].<br><br>These patch definition impl=
ement such as intercepting the standard system HALT to instead be trapped i=
n the Overlay to allow user interaction [retry, abort] and display of all o=
f the latest system call parameters, etc.<br><br>In systems such as the PDP=
-12 LINCtape, a 4K system handler cannot over-ride the automatic initial se=
arch direction guess of always in reverse [which is admittedly usually corr=
ect, but as much as 40 percent of the time this is not true. &nbsp;Performa=
nce can be materially speeded up, etc.]<br><br>When the Logical Console Ove=
rlay is enabled on a PDP-12 LINCtape system, other parts of the handler int=
ercept to instead access a last-transferred block on the corresponding logi=
cal unit. &nbsp;If appropriate, the initial search direction can be forced =
to forward using MTB instructions in extended operatons mode. &nbsp;There i=
sn't enough room in the basic 4K handler to accomplish this, but if extende=
d memory is available and the Overlay is enabled, the handler performce can=
 be palpably improved.<br><br>Thus, the full definition of what compromises=
 all of the code for any particular device [what OS/8 would dub a "system d=
evice handler" has to include these short patch lists defined in the system=
 device handler address space, and some field relocatable interception rout=
ines defined in the Overlay address space.<br><br>I really do not recall, b=
ut I suspect at some point, the source code of the Overlay also had conditi=
onals within it for this purpose, but the newer source code does not. &nbsp=
;This function has been added to the source code of all of the fully suppor=
ted devices, notable ones listed below. &nbsp;Feel free to inquire regardin=
g other devices not mentioned.<br><br>A recently recovered file from 1983 a=
ppears to be the last gasp of an attempt to have all of the major component=
s of P?S/8 not only in memory at the same time, but also from the output of=
 a single assembly.<br><br>Note: &nbsp;The creation of a P?S/8 starter syst=
em is to assemble these same components [and a few additional ones] into in=
dividual binary modules where a system generation routine [essentially a st=
ring of calls to the system device handler] is used to write out all of the=
 components from memory to the designated target system blocks. &nbsp;Progr=
ams are assembled such that they appear to be in the address space they exe=
cute in, but are binary loaded in buffers determined by the assembly proces=
s as defined for system generation. &nbsp;However, in the original oversize=
d version, the only thing gained was local definitions followed through the=
 assembly process being a single unit.<br><br>This was later corrected in a=
 far more globally acceptable way:<br><br>All global definitions must origi=
nate in the keyboard monitor assembly, which today is devoid of actual hand=
ler-related code [just templates and some ZBLOCK sections]. &nbsp;In a few =
places, some definitions actually originate in the Logical Console Overlay,=
 and if they change there, they must be updated in the main PQSMON source f=
ile even if not actually used there.<br><br>A TECO macro has been written [=
called GLOBAL.TEC] [which was also used in the creation of the PR Newswire =
project development] that creates a symbol table from the listing file of t=
he assembly in memory, but only for specific symbols that may be defined wi=
thin a source file within special delimters:<br><br>// &nbsp;SPECIAL SYSTEM=
S SYMBOLS<br><br>equates here<br><br></div><div>// &nbsp;END OF SPECIAL SYS=
TEMS SYMBOLS<br><br>The macro actually only checks for the existence of the=
 first section of comments starting with // followed by equate states [such=
 as SBOOT=3D 7600] ended with a second occurrence of the // comment.<br><br=
>The only restriction is that all lines within the area have to be either b=
lank or equate statements.<br><br>The macro is passed an assembler listing =
file [only the symbols are needed] and the target source file to be updated=
..<br><br>Thus, it is imperative the symbol table of PQSMON define all symb=
ols [which means a few kludges are used, such as definitions that actually =
arise from the Logical Console Overlay and perhaps the P/S/8 BIN utility, n=
ow that these are all separate assemblies. &nbsp;Fortunately, these externa=
l definitions are not likely to change by nature.<br><br>Once all compatibl=
e components have been processed, the files can be assembled individually a=
nd then each can take its proper binary place in the system generation proc=
ess. &nbsp;12K or perhaps 16K is required; future changes will limit this t=
o requiring 12K or perhaps less [depending upon the availability of a prope=
r implementation of the DTORG directive].<br><br>The only problem with what=
 was accomplished is that certain system devices were "left behind" and in =
a few cases, never defined for an overlay.<br><br>It was feared some were e=
ntirely lost such as the TD8E system that uses extended memory for which I =
only had some vague odd recollections of some optimizations over the code a=
s used in most of the OS/8 handlers [which is actually pretty poor].<br><br=
>Eventually a long-forgotten existent TD8E w/MR8E-C ROM system was recovere=
d, and actually booted. &nbsp;It is now theorized that a serious but was ne=
ver addressed, as there was little further contact with actual TD8E hardwar=
e.<br><br>The bug in this system is that there is an attempt to overlay the=
 code with a copy of itself take off the DECtape from the corresponding blo=
cks. &nbsp;this technique works entirely well IF the TC01/08 DECtape contro=
ller is in effect because of the use of DMA.<br><br>In the TD8E ROM system,=
 unfortunately a checksum temporary is designated by the ROM; it cannot be =
moved to a "safe place". &nbsp;As such, any attempt to modify the actual bl=
ock contents renders the system unbootable. &nbsp;[I needed this tape for a=
n expedient piurpose, plus I have a "negative association" with this tape b=
ecause of the personnel involved, but this is beyond any technical discussi=
on.<br><br>I have disassembled other P?S/8 system components before [as wel=
l as code I never wrote; the cosmetic quality and commentary generally exce=
ed that of the original author's.]. &nbsp;this is part of a large document =
with a lot of good documentation that should be split-off and word-processe=
d, however, the code is confirmed as 100% correct for an old release of P?S=
/8 circa 1976 for TC01/08.<br><br>As such, I was confident I could recover =
all of the code on this TD8E w/ROM system and eventually did reproduce the =
source code [including the ROM itself]. &nbsp;It will be interesting to see=
 the "quality" of this disassembly compared to the original code [now recen=
tly possible!].<br><br>I have located the bug and made a partial start on s=
olving the problem, tabling this for time's sake; the SIMH simulator allows=
 debugging the results, etc.<br><br>With regard to TD8E-related issues, it =
is apprarently now nearly a complete recovery, except that it is known the =
code did work with the then-current VT program, which was the non-configura=
ble forerunner of the Logical Console Overlay.<br><br>Thus, I have to disas=
semble that code to determine what are the appropriate interactive intercep=
ts to apply and the appropriate error messages for the generic benefit of t=
he Overlay.<br><br>For brevity I will close at this time, next time a few m=
ore "lost boy" stories and a final summary.</div><div><br></div><div>cjl</d=
iv><div><br><br></div></div></html-blob></div>
